## What Could Go Wrong

### 1. `@Input() pageSize` type correctness
`CandidatePageSize = 20 | 50 | 100` exactly matches the option set. Any
out-of-range value (e.g. a future caller passing an unlisted number) will be
caught by TypeScript at compile time. The runtime guard in
`loadPageSizePreference()` covers values arriving from localStorage that
predate an option-set change.

### 3. Corrupt / out-of-range localStorage value
The `includes(parsed)` guard in `loadPageSizePreference()` handles this: any
value not in `[20, 50, 100]` (including `NaN`, old values like 12 or 10,
stale options) falls through to the @Input check and then the default of 20.

### 4. Page number out of bounds after size change
When the user enlarges page size (e.g. from 12 to 50), they may have been on
page 4. Resetting `pageNumber = 1` in `onPageSizeChange` prevents the server
from receiving a page-number that no longer exists.

### 5. Cache invalidation
After a page-size change, `doSearch(true)` is called with `refresh=true`.
`checkCache` skips cache reads when `refresh=true`, so the server is always
hit. The cache is then repopulated via `cacheResults()` with the new page size.
No explicit cache invalidation is needed.

